Yices relink kit
================

You do not need this kit to install or run Yices
-------------------------------------------------

If you want to use Yices, download the normal Yices distribution archive and
follow the instructions in its README. Nothing in this kit is required to
install Yices, to run the command-line tools, or to link a program against the
shared Yices library. Static-library clients need matching dependency archives:
deps/ supplies these, or you can build compatible archives from the accompanying
canonical dependency-source assets. See the binary distribution README's
"Static-library clients" section.

This kit is provided for relinking. Some of the libraries built into the Yices
binaries are distributed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, which
gives you the right to modify those libraries and rebuild the Yices programs
against your modified versions. The kit contains the material you need to do
that: the Yices object code, the dependency archives in deps/, taken from the
official build, the link commands, and the license texts and notices for the
components the kit redistributes.

The kit does not carry every input the official link used. The ordinary
relink scripts use the machine's C library, startup files and compiler runtime.
REBUILDING.md also describes a separate replacement-glibc route; its compiler
runtime remains a prerequisite. See "What this kit does not do" below.


What is in the kit
------------------

  README                     this file
  MANIFEST.sha256            checksum for every other shipped file
  BUILDINFO                  toolchain, flags, and the production link
                             commands as they were recorded
  LICENSE                    Yices' own license terms
  NOTICES                    the components in this kit and their notices
  licenses/                  license texts that accompany this kit
  executables/               Yices object code for the command-line tools
  library/                   Yices object code for the shared library
  deps/                      the dependency archives used in the official build
  verify-manifest.sh         checks the kit is intact
  relink-executables.sh      rebuilds the four command-line tools
  relink-library.sh          rebuilds the shared library
  REBUILDING.md              recipient source/rebuild/relink instructions
  rebuild-gmp-libpoly.sh     builds/tests replacement libraries privately
  prepare-glibc-source.py    prepares the exact patched Linux glibc source
  relink-with-glibc.sh       explicit replacement-glibc executable link


Which libraries this concerns
-----------------------------

  GNU C Library    Built into the four command-line executables. The shared
                   library does not contain it; it loads the system copy at
                   run time. The ordinary scripts use the host C library;
                   relink-with-glibc.sh selects a recipient-built replacement.
                   See "What this kit does not do" below.

  GMP              Built into yices, yices-smt, yices-smt2, and the shared
                   library. Not present in yices-sat.

  LibPoly          Built into yices, yices-smt, yices-smt2, and the shared
                   library. Not present in yices-sat.

CUDD, CaDiCaL, Kissat, and CryptoMiniSat are also built into yices, yices-smt,
yices-smt2, and the shared library. This kit publishes no relinking material
for them, and replacing them is not what it is for. Their archives are here
because you cannot relink anything without them, and their notices are here
because this kit redistributes those archives. NOTICES in this kit records
what each component is and the terms its own authors distribute it under
where the repository evidence establishes them, and records the gap where it
does not.

The published shared library statically incorporates GMP, LibPoly, CUDD,
CaDiCaL, Kissat, and CryptoMiniSat, and its current link exports those
libraries' global symbols alongside the Yices API. A process that loads
libyices together with another copy of one of those libraries can silently
interpose one copy's symbols on the other; dynamic linking does not isolate
them. Only the interfaces declared in the installed Yices headers are Yices
public API. Do not call or rely on the exported dependency symbols.


What you need before you start
------------------------------

A C compiler driver and a linker for the same target as the published
binaries. The target and the toolchain used for the official build are
recorded in BUILDINFO. A static C library is required for the executables,
which on most distributions is a separate package from the shared one.

The supplied objects include C++ code built by the compiler recorded in
BUILDINFO. The relinking toolchain must therefore provide a compatible C++
runtime; a same-target C compiler alone is not sufficient. In particular, the
current objects require the runtime entry points
`std::__throw_bad_array_new_length()` and `std::ios_base_library_init()`.
The latter was observed as GLIBCXX_3.4.32 in the qualifying libstdc++.
Matching BUILDINFO's GCC 14.2.1 toolchain is the prescribed workflow.

The scripts take CC from the environment and default to cc. The unchanged kit
also passed recipient relinking with GCC 13.3, 15.3, and 16.2. It failed with
GCC 8.5, and its shared library was not usable by an ordinary C API client
with GCC 11.4, 11.5, or 12.2. These observations do not establish an exact
minimum compiler version: distributions can package the C++ runtime
differently. `relink-library.sh` performs a post-link client check and fails
instead of reporting success when the selected runtime is incompatible.

You do not need the Yices source tree, a build directory, or make. The scripts
work from the extracted kit alone.


What this kit does not do
-------------------------

The ordinary relink-executables.sh uses the machine's C library and startup
files; there is no libc archive in deps/. For a recipient-built glibc, use
the separate Linux relink-with-glibc.sh and the complete source-preparation,
build and runtime-verification instructions in REBUILDING.md. It explicitly
selects that glibc's startup objects and libraries, while obtaining the GCC
runtime from your compatible compiler. It installs nothing into the host.
You can also use the supplied objects in your own link command with the
appropriate C-library, startup and toolchain inputs. The convenience scripts
do not restrict that use or certify an arbitrary modified C runtime.

One thing relinking does not change is the version text yices-sat prints. That
text names the GNU C Library, and which text the program carries was settled
when executables/yices_sat.o was compiled rather than when you link it. A
yices-sat relinked on a system whose C library is not the GNU C Library still
prints that text. The scripts link the object you were given; they do not
compile it again.


Step 1: verify the kit

    ./verify-manifest.sh

Do this before you change anything, so that a later failure is clearly your
change and not a bad download.


Step 2: replace a library

REBUILDING.md gives a tested source/build recipe using the dependency archives
published with this release and new private prefixes. Build your modified
version of GMP or LibPoly as a static archive, then either
drop it into deps/ under the existing name or point the script at it. For
example:

    GMP_A=/path/to/my/libgmp.a ./relink-executables.sh

Once you replace a file in deps/, the manifest check will fail, which is
correct and expected. Set SKIP_VERIFY=1 to continue.

These are the inputs relink-executables.sh accepts. It reads nothing else from
the environment.

    CC             compatible compiler driver, default cc; see Requirements
    CFLAGS         compile and link flags, see Step 3
    STATIC_FLAG    default -static
    BIN_LDFLAGS    extra link flags, empty by default
    LIBYICES_A     default executables/libyices.a
    GMP_A          default deps/libgmp-static.a
    LIBPOLY_A      default deps/libpoly.a
    CUDD_A         default deps/libcudd.a
    CADICAL_A      default deps/libcadical.a
    CMS_A          default deps/libcryptominisat5.a
    KISSAT_A       default deps/libkissat_yices.a
    SKIP_VERIFY    set to 1 to bypass the manifest check

The first argument is the output directory. It defaults to ./relinked.
Within the kit, only that reserved relinked directory is allowed. For a custom
location, use a directory outside the kit with an existing parent, for example
../my-relinked-yices. Both scripts may use that same external directory.
Do not disable manifest verification merely to allow a custom output path.

relink-library.sh accepts the same set with four differences. It has no
STATIC_FLAG and no BIN_LDFLAGS. It reads LIBYICES_PIC_A rather than
LIBYICES_A, defaulting to library/libyices-pic.a. Its GMP_A and LIBPOLY_A
default to the position-independent archives deps/libgmp-pic.a and
deps/libpicpoly.a. It adds SONAME, default libyices.so.2.8, and LIBNAME,
default libyices.so.2.8.0.


Step 3: relink

    ./relink-executables.sh            builds into ./relinked
    ./relink-library.sh                builds into ./relinked

Each script prints the command it runs before running it. Treat the
corresponding link-* entry in BUILDINFO as the reference command. The printed
command is not identical to that entry. These are the differences.

  CC defaults to cc. BUILDINFO's cc field records the configure compiler
  command gcc -std=gnu17. The script treats CC as one executable name, so set
  CC=gcc rather than assigning that whole field to CC. Put -std=gnu17 at the
  front of CFLAGS if you want the printed link command to retain it.

  CFLAGS defaults to
  -fvisibility=hidden -O3 -fomit-frame-pointer -fno-stack-protector.
  BUILDINFO records those same four flags with -Wall and -Wredundant-decls
  inserted after -fvisibility=hidden, the whole preceded by a -ffile-prefix-map
  option, which rewrote the source paths recorded in the
  object files when they were compiled. The two warning flags control
  diagnostics and do not change the generated code. BUILDINFO carries the
  -ffile-prefix-map value that was used.

  The scripts resolve the kit to an absolute path and print an absolute
  pathname for every object file and archive. BUILDINFO records the same
  files as kit-relative paths, such as executables/yices.o and
  deps/libcudd.a.

  The scripts write into the output directory, so the printed command gives an
  output path under that directory, such as -o ./relinked/yices. BUILDINFO
  records a bare output name, -o yices.

Setting CC=gcc and setting CFLAGS to -std=gnu17 followed by the value in
BUILDINFO's cflags field closes the first two groups of differences. The last
two remain. They follow from where you unpacked the kit and where you sent the
output, and no setting changes them, so compare the two commands input by
input rather than as strings. -std=gnu17 and -ffile-prefix-map affect
compilation rather than linking, so passing them here changes nothing in the
output.


Two things that are easy to get wrong
--------------------------------------

This Linux kit contains two GMP archives and they are not interchangeable.
deps/libgmp-static.a is an optimized build used by the command-line
executables. deps/libgmp-pic.a is a position-independent build with the
assembly routines disabled, used by the shared library. Apple Silicon kits
have the same two-build arrangement. The Intel macOS kit instead has one fat,
position-independent GMP archive that both links use, as its own README and
BUILDINFO state. The executables also use the normal deps/libpoly.a archive,
while the shared library uses deps/libpicpoly.a. These normal and PIC LibPoly
archives are not interchangeable.
GMP's x86-64 assembly code is not position-independent and cannot be linked
into a shared object. If you supply your own GMP or LibPoly for the shared
library, configure it for position-independent code.

yices-sat is linked differently from the other three tools. It contains
neither GMP nor LibPoly, so if you modified only one of those, yices-sat is
unaffected and the published binary remains correct. Its link list still names
the CUDD, CryptoMiniSat, CaDiCaL and Kissat archives, because the production
rule for that binary names them too. A static linker takes only the archive
members that something refers to, and the published yices-sat carries code
from none of those four. NOTICES section 3.1 records that placement. The C
library is built into yices-sat as it is into the other three, and the scripts
take it from the machine you relink on. relink-executables.sh rebuilds all
four whichever library you replaced.


The shared library
------------------

relink-library.sh sets the shared object name to libyices.so.2.8 and creates
the two symbolic links a normal installation expects:

    libyices.so.2.8   -> libyices.so.2.8.0
    libyices.so       -> libyices.so.2.8

Check the result with:

    readelf -dW relinked/libyices.so.2.8.0 | grep -E 'SONAME|NEEDED'

The shared library incorporates the PIC static CUDD archive supplied in this
kit. It does not require a CUDD shared library at run time.


Installing what you built
-------------------------

Copy the relinked files over the ones in an unpacked Yices distribution, or
place them ahead of the installed copies on your path or library search path.
The kit does not install anything and does not modify your system.


License texts and notices
-------------------------

The licenses/ directory holds license texts for some of the components this kit
redistributes. It is not a complete set. licenses/ has no file for CUDD, and
NOTICES reproduces CUDD's terms in full instead. Version 3 of
the Lesser General Public License incorporates the General Public License by
reference, which is why both of those are present. The MIT and Apache
License 2.0 texts are present because components in deps/ are distributed under
them, and NOTICES records which component each applies to. The exact CPython
3.4.0a3 license and copyright notice accompany the minimum-run sequence
described in NOTICES section 1.4. The BSD text is Yices' own license, which
this kit also carries as LICENSE.

The GNU General Public License version 2 text is present for the GPL-2.0-only
files in the separately published CryptoMiniSat source asset, not for any
archive in deps/. NOTICES section 3.9 records that exact scope.

The set depends on the platform. A kit for a build that contains the GNU C
Library also carries the Lesser General Public License version 2.1; a kit for a
platform whose C library comes from the operating system does not, because no
C library is built in.

NOTICES, in this kit, records each third-party component the kit
redistributes, where it came from, and the terms its own authors distribute it
under where the repository evidence establishes them, and records the gap
where it does not. It reproduces the copyright notices supplied by CUDD,
CaDiCaL, Kissat, and CryptoMiniSat, whose archives are in deps/. For GMP and
LibPoly itself it records that the source carries copyright notices in
individual files rather than one notice for the project.
